\fBw\fR shows who is logged in to the system and what each user is
currently doing. The output begins with the same header line as
\fBuptime\fR(1) (current time, system uptime, user count, load
averages), followed by one row per active login session.
.PP
The session list is built from \fI/var/run/utmp\fR (see \fButmp\fR(5))
to enumerate logged-in users, then correlated with \fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR
(see \fBproc_pid_stat\fR(5)) to identify each session's foreground
process and its accumulated CPU time. With \fB\-u\fR the foreground
lookup is skipped and \fBw\fR reports the login process itself rather
than whatever it ran.
.PP
A positional \fIUSER\fR argument restricts the output to one user's
sessions. \fB\-s\fR collapses the table to just \fIUSER\fR /
\fITTY\fR / \fIIDLE\fR / \fIWHAT\fR. \fB\-f\fR adds a \fIFROM\fR
column showing where the session was opened from (remote host,
display, or "login screen"). \fB\-h\fR omits the header line, useful
in scripts.
